1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,347
Most AI demos begin with the same question:
what can an agent do?

2
00:00:05,497 --> 00:00:08,375
Protocol Mirror begins with a harder one.

3
00:00:08,525 --> 00:00:10,993
What should an agent never decide?

4
00:00:11,277 --> 00:00:12,317
AI assembles evidence.

5
00:00:12,467 --> 00:00:13,507
A human decides.

6
00:00:13,657 --> 00:00:18,510
This is Protocol Mirror, a live WebMCP workspace
for clinical-trial transparency.

7
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:22,180
Trial registries show what researchers planned.

8
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:25,459
Publications show what readers eventually see.

9
00:00:25,609 --> 00:00:34,998
Protocol Mirror places those records side by side,
then gives an agent typed access to stable outcome identifiers and exact source spans.

10
00:00:35,549 --> 00:00:41,979
The agent can inspect the evidence, stage a structured mapping,
and bring the relevant human checkpoint into view.

11
00:00:42,129 --> 00:00:46,667
Every suggestion cites both sides, labels uncertainty,
and explains the mismatch.

12
00:00:47,379 --> 00:00:51,753
But the agent cannot accept or reject its own proposal.

13
00:00:51,903 --> 00:00:57,152
That authority exists only here, in the interface,
for a human reviewer.

14
00:00:57,652 --> 00:01:02,543
Once the reviewer decides, the capability surface
changes from six tools to seven.

15
00:01:02,693 --> 00:01:08,337
The new receipt tool exports only reviewed work,
exact evidence locators, and the decision trail.

16
00:01:08,487 --> 00:01:09,992
Staged suggestions stay out.

17
00:01:10,788 --> 00:01:17,453
Two additional tools retrieve current ClinicalTrials.gov
and PubMed records through bounded, validated adapters.

18
00:01:17,603 --> 00:01:23,875
A deterministic fictional case keeps the judging flow reliable,
even when a public source is unavailable.

19
00:01:24,631 --> 00:01:28,543
Protocol Mirror makes AI useful precisely where
it should never have final authority.
